aken to encourage and empower young people to participate in the political process. One of the main reasons for the youth's disengagement from politics is the perceived lack of representation and relevance. Often, political discourse in many countries fails to address the concerns and aspirations of young people. This disconnect leads to disillusionment and apathy among the youth. To overcome this, political parties and leaders need to actively engage with young people, listen to their voices, and incorporate their perspectives into policy-making. By doing so, the youth will feel valued and encouraged to participate actively in the political arena. Education also plays a crucial role in fostering youth participation in politics. Schools and universities should include political education in their curriculum to cultivate an understanding of democratic processes, constitutional rights, and the importance of civic engagement. By equipping young people with the knowledge and skills necessary for active citizenship, we can create a generation of politically aware and empowered individuals. Furthermore, technological advancements have provided a platform for young people to voice their opinions and engage in political discourse. Social media platforms have emerged as powerful tools for political mobilization and activism. Young people can use these platforms to raise awareness about social and political issues, organize grassroots movements, and hold policymakers accountable. Governments and political parties should recognize the potential of social media and create spaces where young people can participate and contribute meaningfully. In addition to this, mentorship programs and internships can encourage young people to explore careers in politics. By providing opportunities for hands-on experience, mentoring, and networking, we can nurture the next generation of political leaders. Political parties should establish youth wings and create platforms for young people to ac